A||||5||Gokart||Headliner question||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||04-21-2000||09:43 PM||||Noticed the headliner in my parts car looks much better than the one in my GT. Could use a little cleaning though, but what could I use on it that won't unglue the cloth material from it causing it to sag later on? TIA for replys.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||04-21-2000||09:53 PM||||Gokart, Just replace it. I did a nice job on my 87 coupe, Everything you need is at the fabric store. Price = less than $20.00 TOTAL and easy to do. I'm sure some other members have done this also. The gray fabric matched as close as factory.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Gokart||04-21-2000||10:12 PM||||What kind of glue did you use for the fabric against the headliner itself that didn't bleed through the fabric? Thanks Bill.||||reg||5
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||04-21-2000||11:19 PM||||It's foam backed headliner fabric, they have the foam spray adhesive, 3M also makes a spray adhesive for foam. I think it was Elmar's that I got from the fabric store.||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo86se||04-22-2000||10:55 AM||||Hey Gokart, I got a tip from someone on the Forum (I wish I could remember who, to give credit), but after you take the headliner out, take a broom and "sweep" the old glue off. Next, get yourself a couple cans of quick-drying Polyurethane and brush it on both sides of the headliner. It makes the headliner very rigid! It may take an extra day or two, but well worth it!<BR>Good luck and have fun!!!!||||reg||10
Z||000005||Gokart||04-23-2000||07:52 AM||||That's a great idea! Thanks, both of you guys! I'll have a new looking headliner soon <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
